data: {"id":"chatcmpl-e820d851-9653-452b-8be4-61f63c9cf9aa","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795553188,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"The key insight is that `COMMIT` applies changes and closes the transaction, meaning only other *open* transactions' snapshots need preservation. After a transaction's `COMMIT`, its snapshot is no longer required. This simplifies snapshotting logic by minimizing the number of pinned epochs. Snapshot lifetimes are limited to the duration of their respective transactions.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-e820d851-9653-452b-8be4-61f63c9cf9aa","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795553188,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"The core idea is to avoid incrementing the global `stateEpoch` unnecessarily.  The global epoch should only advance if a currently open transaction requires the previous state; otherwise, the current epoch can be re-used.  At transaction `COMMIT`, check if other open transactions' snapshots still reference the current `stateRoot`. If not, the current epoch can be reused.  This minimizes unnecessary epoch increments and facilitates in-place mutation of nodes.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-e820d851-9653-452b-8be4-61f63c9cf9aa","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795553188,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"`BEGIN`'s), and then T3 `ABORT`s? It seems we always need to increment the `stateEpoch` at `COMMIT`, because even aborted transactions can impact snapshot lifecycles.  Reconsidering this logic, there might be cases where the epoch can be reused. Need to rigorously analyze the interplay between open transactions and the global state epoch, especially for aborted transactions.\n\nThe key question is when `stateEpoch` can be safely advanced, and whether aborted transactions invalidate epoch reuse opportunities. The current assumption of incrementing on every commit might be overly conservative. This requires a deeper dive into the specific scenarios of open and closed transaction interactions.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

