data: {"id":"chatcmpl-e820d851-9653-452b-8be4-61f63c9cf9aa","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795553188,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"Considering a scenario where transaction T1 `BEGIN`s, then T2 and T3 `BEGIN` and `COMMIT`, followed by T1 `COMMIT`. Analyzing how these commits interact with `stateEpoch` and snapshot lifetimes to avoid unnecessary increments. There's a subtle interaction to track: T6 could BEGIN at epoch 2, T7 PUT and COMMIT incrementing the epoch to 3, then T6 closes.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-e820d851-9653-452b-8be4-61f63c9cf9aa","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795553188,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"Analyzing scenarios where transactions are committed with empty write lists. Considering how this impacts `stateEpoch` management and snapshot lifetimes, especially in cases with multiple concurrent transactions. The central question is whether a transaction committing with no modifications requires a state epoch increment.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-e820d851-9653-452b-8be4-61f63c9cf9aa","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795553188,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"The core invariant focuses on epoch management for in-place mutation and snapshotting consistency. A transaction commits iff it has writes. Before any write at commit, if any open transaction's snapshot root equals the current state root, increment the state epoch. Only nodes with matching epochs are mutated; others are copied. This maintains snapshot integrity during concurrent transactions.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-e820d851-9653-452b-8be4-61f63c9cf9aa","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795553188,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"If `stateEpoch` becomes `E`, `stateRoot` changes. No open transaction can hold a previous `snapshotRoot` containing a node with `epoch == stateEpoch`. The only possible open transaction's snapshot containing nodes with `epoch == stateEpoch` is `stateRoot` itself, proving a highly memory-efficient commit strategy. This shows a single `stateEpoch` increment can accommodate 500,000 transactions, with zero extra node allocations beyond the initial data size.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

