data: {"id":"chatcmpl-e820d851-9653-452b-8be4-61f63c9cf9aa","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795553188,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"The challenge lies in handling a scenario where a single transaction (T) remains open while numerous other transactions (U) begin and commit, with T potentially reading from ranges overlapping U's writes. Conflict detection must be efficient, ideally O(log N) per operation, avoiding an O(reads * commits) complexity. The key is in R22: conflict happens if a transaction U committed after T's BEGIN and before T's COMMIT and their keys overlap.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-e820d851-9653-452b-8be4-61f63c9cf9aa","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795553188,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"The critical point is that a transaction's read set conflicts if *any* committed transaction's writes fall between the read transaction's BEGIN and COMMIT, regardless of the read operation's log position. This simplifies conflict detection by focusing on the transaction commit order and key overlap, not the precise timing of reads within a transaction. The read operations act on the snapshot taken at the transaction's BEGIN, which defines the read set for conflict analysis. This simplifies conflict analysis significantly: it is not the read log position, but the commit log position of the write that matters.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-e820d851-9653-452b-8be4-61f63c9cf9aa","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795553188,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"`lastWriteCommitSeq(K)` is the largest `U.commitSeq` of any transaction that wrote to key `K`.  `lastWriteCommitSeq(K)` allows efficient conflict detection.  `T` conflicts with a transaction `U` if and only if any key `K` read by `T` was modified by a transaction `U` with `U.commitSeq > T.beginCommitSeq`.  The `commitSeq` represents a global monotonically increasing transaction ID.\n\nTo determine if `T` conflicts, check if `T.beginCommitSeq` is less than `lastWriteCommitSeq(K)` for ANY `K` read in `T`.  The snapshot mechanism guarantees that no conflicts occur for reads.  The key optimization is that no reads conflict with writes committed *before* the transaction began.  Only writes committed during the transactions.\n\nThe implementation tracks `lastWriteCommitSeq(K)` for each key. During `BEGIN`, the current global `commitSeq` becomes `beginCommitSeq` for that transaction.  `COMMIT` increments a global commit sequence counter. Before commit, the `writeSet` must be checked for conflicts with other open transactions, not writes already committed before the `BEGIN` epoch.\n\nThe overall approach is to keep a commit log with a single global commit sequence number. On `BEGIN`, store the commit sequence. On `COMMIT`, increment it, check for conflicts (keys updated since `BEGIN`), and update the committed state.  The current snapshot approach seems correct and efficient.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-e820d851-9653-452b-8be4-61f63c9cf9aa","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795553188,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"The equivalence of the conflict conditions is now proven using `lastWriteCommitSeq(K)`, checking it at the time of transaction commit.  The read set conflict condition, verified with the derived equality, is that a key `K` conflicts if `lastWriteCommitSeq(K)` is greater than `T.beginCommitSeq`.  The range read conflict condition needs verification now. The key question is whether a range read also conflicts under the `lastWriteCommitSeq(K)` condition. The goal is to apply similar logic to range reads.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

