data: {"id":"chatcmpl-e820d851-9653-452b-8be4-61f63c9cf9aa","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795553188,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"Zero (`0`) is the empty slot sentinel in the open-addressing integer hash table. A primitive `IntSet` will replace `HashSet<Long>` to reduce memory footprint by avoiding object allocations. In Phase 1, the code can count `beginCount` (or intact records) during the intact record scan. The `beginCount` value is used to determine the `IntSet`'s initial capacity, allocating the required size for the integer array, preventing extra allocations if no records exist. The `IntSet` avoids boxed `Long` allocations since we're just checking for ID existence; we do not iterate over it. This optimization eliminates significant memory overhead.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-e820d851-9653-452b-8be4-61f63c9cf9aa","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795553188,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"The requirement specifies Java Strings, where each byte maps to a character. Java's compact strings optimization, introduced in Java 9, directly copies bytes to the internal string representation, making transcoding unnecessary and efficient. This intrinsic optimization can be leveraged to create strings from byte arrays, especially when bytes are validated within the ASCII range. For keys with length zero, the empty string (\"\") is returned directly. This ensures compliance with the single-file constraint, the byte-to-char mapping, and performance characteristics.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-e820d851-9653-452b-8be4-61f63c9cf9aa","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795553188,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"Checksum calculation optimization hinges on modular arithmetic properties.  The core insight: reducing intermediate sums modulo 255 at intervals doesn't change the final checksum, allowing chunk-wise modular reduction.  This optimization replaces repeated modulo operations with additions, significantly boosting performance.  The logic now needs validation to confirm the equivalency with the original checksum calculation for precise byte-level equivalence.  This verification is crucial to ensure deterministic behavior.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-e820d851-9653-452b-8be4-61f63c9cf9aa","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795553188,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"The checksum calculation simplifies dramatically: since the intermediate sums fit within a 32-bit integer, modulo reduction can be postponed until the end of the entire byte block. This removes modulo operations within the loop, significantly improving performance without sacrificing correctness. The mathematical proof verifies that calculating `s1` and `s2` without intermediate modulo operations results in equivalent checksums when a final modulo operation is applied. This is a critical optimization for performance.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

