data: {"id":"chatcmpl-e820d851-9653-452b-8be4-61f63c9cf9aa","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795553188,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"The code now prioritizes efficient handling of range operations. A crucial optimization involves omitting the storage of ranges where the lower bound exceeds the upper bound (`lo > hi`), since these ranges cannot conflict per R22. This design choice maintains the mathematical equivalence.  The emphasis is on processing only valid ranges (`lo <= hi`).\n\nAddressing the `BEGIN` rule requirements (R17, R18): If `BEGIN` records with new transaction IDs pass R17 but then fail R18 (due to exceeding the maximum open transactions), further investigation is needed to handle this edge case. The goal is to ensure the correct state is reached even in error scenarios.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-e820d851-9653-452b-8be4-61f63c9cf9aa","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795553188,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"Duplicate transaction ID checks (R17) must precede open transaction limit (R18). The logic correctly enforces this order: R17 (`DUPLICATE_TXN`) is checked first. The `seenTxns` set now correctly tracks valid transaction IDs, reflecting that the records must have been a prior `BEGIN`. The `CHECKPOINT` transaction only references open transaction IDs. Validating the precedence of checks R17 and R18. The current implementation prioritizes R17 over R18 as specified by rule P4.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

