data: {"id":"chatcmpl-e820d851-9653-452b-8be4-61f63c9cf9aa","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795553188,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"Conflict detection during `COMMIT` requires removing the committing transaction from the open transactions list and then checking for conflicts. The process involves comparing read keys against `keyLastCommitSeq` and checking range reads against `writeSeqRoot`. For each read key, the code checks if `lastSeq` is more recent than the transaction's `beginSeq`.\n\nRange conflict detection now calls `hasWriteInRange`, which efficiently checks for writes in overlapping ranges. The goal is to detect whether any other transaction committed writes to keys that the current transaction read. The algorithm is complete.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-e820d851-9653-452b-8be4-61f63c9cf9aa","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795553188,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"The conflict detection now has an O(1) early exit: If the `commitSeq` hasn't advanced beyond the transaction's `beginSeq`, no conflict checks are performed, providing a significant optimization.  This avoids unnecessary iterations through read keys.  Crucially, a transaction does not conflict with its own writes within the same transaction due to the commit sequence check.\n\nThe conflict logic also correctly handles the scenario where a transaction `U` begins before `T` and commits after `T`'s begin but before T's commit.  R22's definition of conflict correctly captures the interaction. Further investigation reveals no issues.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-e820d851-9653-452b-8be4-61f63c9cf9aa","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795553188,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"The conflict condition in R22 hinges on the commit record's position, not the begin record. The `commitSeq` at `T`'s `BEGIN` (T.beginCommitSeq) versus `U`'s `commitSeq` at `U`'s `COMMIT` directly reflects the correct chronological order, ensuring conflict detection only considers commits within the critical time window. R22 clarifies that conflict is based on keys at the time of commit, effectively handling rollbacks by only considering committed writes.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-e820d851-9653-452b-8be4-61f63c9cf9aa","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795553188,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"The write list defines the transaction's written keys, regardless of the net effect on state. Even if a PUT is followed by a DELETE of the same key within a transaction, the key is still considered written. This is crucial for conflict detection and ensuring that writes are tracked even if they ultimately have no effect on the final state.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-e820d851-9653-452b-8be4-61f63c9cf9aa","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795553188,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"The key insight is that even if a key is PUT and immediately DELETE'd within a transaction, it still registers as a write for conflict detection according to R22. If transaction `U` is aborted due to either an `ABORT` record or a conflict, `U`'s writes are not committed, `commitSeq` isn't incremented, and they are not added to `keyLastCommitSeq` or `writeSeqRoot`. For transaction `T` which does NOT conflict: (1) Increment `commitSeq++`. (2) If `txn.writeCount > 0`, check if any remaining open transaction's snapshot root equals `stateRoot`.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

