data: {"id":"chatcmpl-e820d851-9653-452b-8be4-61f63c9cf9aa","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795553188,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"The commit sequence (`commitSeq`) increments only if writes exist, and then `stateEpoch` may be advanced. If any open transaction's snapshot root references the current state, `stateEpoch` increments. During write processing within a transaction, each key's last commit sequence is updated. If a key is written multiple times within the transaction, only the final update matters for conflict detection. The implementation focuses on updating the key-sequence mappings only for final writes. This maintains consistent state and accurate conflict detection.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-e820d851-9653-452b-8be4-61f63c9cf9aa","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795553188,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"The `StateNode` structure confirms the correct fields for the persistent order-statistic AVL tree. This includes `key`, `val`, `left`, `right`, `height`, `size`, and `epoch`. These are now all present and correct in the implementation. This confirms we can use these.\n\nTree 2: Write-Sequence AVL Tree (`WriteSeqNode`)\nFields of `WriteSeqNode`:\nprivate static class WriteSeqNode {\n    final String key;\n    final int lastWriteSeq;\n    WriteSeqNode left;\n    WriteSeqNode right;\n    int height;\n    int maxSeq;\n\n    WriteSeqNode(String key, int lastWriteSeq) {\n        this.key = key;\n        this.lastWriteSeq = lastWriteSeq;\n        this.maxSeq = lastWriteSeq;\n        this.height = 1;\n        this.size = 1;\n    }\n\n}\nThe `WriteSeqNode` structure confirms all fields for the auxiliary write-sequence tree. The keys and other metadata are used. The fields include key, `lastWriteSeq`, `left`, `right`, `height`, and `maxSeq`.  This confirms it tracks the maximum commit sequence in the subtree. This AVL tree is also verified.\n\nThe AVL trees are now specified, and the fields are validated. This confirms each key is present and correct.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-e820d851-9653-452b-8be4-61f63c9cf9aa","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795553188,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"The `StateNode` structure and helper methods (`height`, `size`, `ensureMutable`, `updateState`) are now validated, with the rotation methods under inspection. The structure adheres to a persistent AVL tree with correct fields: key, value, left, right, height, size, and epoch, ensuring consistent structure management. The epoch ensures node mutability and persistent structure integrity.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-e820d851-9653-452b-8be4-61f63c9cf9aa","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795553188,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"The rebalancing operations on the AVL tree require careful consideration of node mutability and the `epoch`. Within `rebalanceState`, and specifically in single and double rotations like `rotateRight` and `rotateLeft`, the algorithm only modifies the rotated nodes (e.g., `x` and `y`).  It is possible that the nodes that are being rotated could have an epoch that is less than the current epoch, specifically, when the deleted subtree is smaller than the remaining subtrees. This needs further inspection. The nodes can have an `epoch` less than the current epoch.\n\nThe recursion deletes in `y.right` might not affect `x`, which leads to an inconsistent `epoch`. Left-Right double rotations, in `rotateLeft(x, epoch)`, also modifies `x` and `x.right` nodes, which were also not visited.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-e820d851-9653-452b-8be4-61f63c9cf9aa","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795553188,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"The critical fix is to call `ensureMutable` within `rotateRight` and `rotateLeft`. Without ensuring node mutability before rotation, a delete in a rotated subtree could inadvertently mutate a shared node, leading to snapshot corruption. `ensureMutable` guarantees the nodes being rotated are safe to modify.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

