data: {"id":"chatcmpl-e820d851-9653-452b-8be4-61f63c9cf9aa","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795553188,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"The `putState` function's optimization is confirmed: when the key already exists and the value matches, the function returns the existing node without modification. The case where the key doesn't exist is handled in the recursive calls and `ensureMutable` guarantees the safety of rotations by creating a copy if needed. In `removeState`, the requirement to not modify the state if the key is not in the tree is verified: the function will return `n` and not do anything.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-e820d851-9653-452b-8be4-61f63c9cf9aa","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795553188,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"The `removeState` function needs refinement to handle in-place mutation correctly during deletion, especially when the target key isn't found. The crucial case: if the left child is mutated in-place by `removeState` and returned, we must ensure `n`'s height and size are still recomputed and up-to-date by calling `ensureMutable(n, epoch)` on the way down. The `ensureMutable` call guarantees node modifications are safe for snapshot consistency. It is important to remember to call `ensureMutable` on any node.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

