data: {"id":"chatcmpl-e820d851-9653-452b-8be4-61f63c9cf9aa","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795553188,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"Ensuring immutability during node deletion within `removeState`: `ensureMutable` is called on the nodes `n` during traversal.  Handles the deletion of the node itself. When no `left` is present, `n.right` is returned; if `right` is `null`, `n.left` is returned. In the case where both child nodes are available, the minimal successor is sought from the right subtree. The `removeMinState` implementation must also call `ensureMutable` to maintain snapshot integrity.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-e820d851-9653-452b-8be4-61f63c9cf9aa","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795553188,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"The AVL tree rotations are now fully validated for persistence. The analysis focuses on scenarios including `putState` and `removeState`, with `removeMinState` under scrutiny. Confirmed that the `StateNode`'s final `key` and `epoch` guarantee data integrity. Every mutation is performed either within a newly created node, or through the safe `ensureMutable` method, ensuring no data corruption. The `removeMinState` and `removeState` calls consistently apply `ensureMutable`, making them immutable. No mutations happen without the epoch match. The persistence is provable.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

