data: {"id":"chatcmpl-e820d851-9653-452b-8be4-61f63c9cf9aa","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795553188,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"The initial plan was a `HashSet` to deduplicate read keys, but `ArrayList` offers better performance and memory efficiency by leveraging the object pool and avoiding extra object allocations during `.clear()`. `ArrayList` can hold a max of 500,000 strings (2 MB), whereas `HashSet` can allocate ~16 MB. The existing `Txn` pooling makes `ArrayList` with `.clear()` a superior, memory-optimized solution for tracking read keys, even with many transactions.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-e820d851-9653-452b-8be4-61f63c9cf9aa","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795553188,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"Initial analysis of the `RANGE` operation (Type 10) confirms that rules R13, R14, and R15 are executed directly on the byte array log. Rule R19 (`TXN_NOT_OPEN`) is checked before R19e (`RANGE_MISMATCH`). The implementation efficiently decodes the lower (`lo`) and upper (`hi`) bounds of the range, as well as the expected count from the log.\n\nThe code now checks R19e: it calculates `actualCount` using `countLessOrEqual` and `countLess` and adjusting it by processing the transaction's write list to account for modified keys within the range. The write list iteration correctly handles overrides to ensure an accurate count.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

